  {
   "title": "Blast Beats and Standards",
   "logline": "A jazz guitarist chasing a jazz-metal fusion sees his triumph turn to ruin when an injury ends his playing, and he must find his way back to his bandmates and a reason to keep believing in his sound.",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "index": 2,
     "text": "Mike's quartet sold out the Blue Lantern, though the fee barely covered bus fare and he doubted his metal experiment."
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "text": "Buoyed by the sellout, Mike rewrote standards into a blast-beat hybrid, convinced the fusion would redefine jazz."
    },
    {
     "index": 4,
     "text": "Half the crowd walked out during the first fusion set, and the club cancelled Mike's Friday residency, yet he insisted they'd come around."
    },
    {
     "index": 5,
     "text": "A torn tendon ended Mike's playing; he burned his fusion charts while his bandmates' benefit concert and a university salary kept him housed."
    },
    {
     "index": 6,
     "text": "Mike sat silent at every rehearsal, unable to play, as the quartet voted him permanent arranger and co-owner of their publishing."
    },
    {
     "index": 7,
     "text": "Mike's royalty check arrived the same week he left the quartet, convinced a one-handed fusion album could still prove the doubters wrong."
    }
